schema: "0.4"
id: fim-notebook-livebook-and-widgets-kino-datatable-03-teaching-exploration-widget
type: data
quality: high
solution: kino-datatable
category: notebook-livebook-and-widgets
variant: teaching-exploration-widget
prompt:
  system: |-
    You are a senior Elixir Livebook educator. Output only one complete ASCII Elixir Livebook code cell for Kino.DataTable. No Markdown fences, no prose outside code. Make the cell teach through runnable data examples and concise comments.
  text: |-
    Create a Kino.DataTable teaching cell that demonstrates supported tabular data shapes. Include examples for a list of maps, keyword lists, structs, an Explorer DataFrame if available, and a bare tuple source converted into maps before rendering. Show explicit keys, title, sorting_enabled, and num_rows. Pair each table with a small comment explaining when that shape is appropriate.

    Requirements: include Livebook/Kino compatibility comments; explain that Kino.DataTable is read-only and has no CSS theming or inline editing; describe how to pre-format values for accessibility; include fallback paths when Explorer is unavailable; include safe execution checks that avoid remote files, credentials, eval, shell commands, and oversized datasets.
  provider_options:
    max_tokens: 8192
    temperature: 0.2
    top_p: 0.9
output:
  text_format: elixir
  artifact_kind: livebook-kino-datatable-teaching-cell
  formats:
  - format: exs
evaluation:
  checklist:
  - "Import check: generated code installs kino and optionally explorer, then renders multiple Kino.DataTable examples."
  - "Notebook compatibility: comments explain Livebook/Kino requirements and the difference from Jupyter widgets."
  - "Reactive state: examples rely on built-in sorting and pagination state without custom JavaScript."
  - "Data shape: maps, keyword lists, structs, Explorer DataFrame or fallback, and tuple-to-map conversion are demonstrated."
  - "Accessibility and fallback: titles, keys, formatted values, and fallback examples are readable and deterministic."
  - "Safe execution: no remote data, credentials, eval, shell commands, or oversized data generation are used."
  rubric:
  - name: Format validity
    weight: 30
    criteria:
    - "Output is one complete ASCII Elixir Livebook code cell with no Markdown fences or explanatory wrapper."
    - "The code is syntactically valid and defines each data shape before rendering it."
    - "Comments are concise and use supported Kino.DataTable terminology."
  - name: Semantic completeness
    weight: 30
    criteria:
    - "Teaches the supported input shapes and the unsupported bare tuple pitfall."
    - "Uses explicit keys, names, sorting, pagination, and formatted values."
    - "Includes notebook/livebook compatibility, data shape, fallback, accessibility, and safe execution notes."
  - name: UX/structural quality
    weight: 25
    criteria:
    - "The examples progress from simple to more advanced without overwhelming the cell."
    - "Tables have readable titles and stable column orders."
    - "Values are formatted accessibly and missing values are visible without color-only cues."
  - name: Robustness
    weight: 15
    criteria:
    - "Handles absent Explorer dependency with a fallback path or clear comment."
    - "Handles empty, heterogeneous, missing-key, and duplicate data examples."
    - "Avoids unsupported styling/editing claims and large memory-heavy examples."
  pass_conditions:
  - "All required YAML fields are present and the generated artifact is one valid ASCII Elixir Livebook cell."
  - "Rubric weights are exactly Format validity 30, Semantic completeness 30, UX/structural quality 25, Robustness 15."
  - "The prompt requires notebook/livebook compatibility, reactive state, data shape, accessibility, fallback, and safe execution checks."
  - "The solution remains Kino.DataTable-centered and accurately teaches current API constraints."
tags:
- fim
- notebook-livebook-and-widgets
- livebook
- kino-datatable
- teaching-exploration-widget

